Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

ChFi3d_FilDS: the end of a closed stripe is its start: the index of its last
points is the index of its first (ChFi3d_MakeExtremities), a vertex index
when the first common point is a vertex. The type of the interference at
the end was taken from the last common point, which a walk may end without
identifying with that vertex; a vertex index then went in as the index of a
point, and ChFi3d_Builder::Compute failed in
TopOpeBRepDS_DataStructure::Point (a closed blend rolling on a closed edge,
from the edge overflow of the previous patch, reaches it). The type now
comes from the first common point too.

Test: none, because no small body was found that reaches the branch at
the port's tip (mitcad#2121, from the code; nothing was run for it). It
needs a closed stripe whose first common point on a side is a vertex
while the walk ends its last one, at the same point one period on,
without that vertex. The case it was written for, the foot of a round
step rounded higher than the step, the ball rolling on the closed top rim
(geometry/tests/test_fillet_overflow.cpp,
test_closed_roundings_roll_on_a_rim), starts on the rim's vertex, where
the seams of the step's side and of the foot meet, and builds without the
patch at the tip (shown 2026-11-20 by tools/occt-port-proof.py): the end
there is taken for the vertex too, or the start is not, since later
patches handle a closed rolling stripe starting on a seam vertex (0152)
and make a closed stripe's end the vertex where its contact runs along a
face's edge (0321). A rim whose vertex lies off the spine's start puts
neither end on a vertex; a run-based search for a body that starts on a
vertex and ends off it is still to be made.
